\title{Statistical conventions in ivreg2r}
\description{
\code{ivreg2r} is a translation of Stata's \code{ivreg2}, and it follows Stata's
statistical conventions rather than those of R's \code{\link[=lm]{lm()}}/\code{\link[=vcov]{vcov()}} ecosystem.
This topic collects those conventions in one place. The API itself is
idiomatic R (formula interface, tidyverse-friendly output); only the
\emph{statistical} choices below lean on Stata. See \code{\link[=ivreg2]{ivreg2()}} for the
estimation function and \link{ivreg2r-glossary} for the diagnostic acronyms
printed by \code{\link[=summary]{summary()}}.
}
\section{Default VCE and small-sample corrections}{


\code{vcov} defaults to \code{"iid"} (classical) and \code{small} defaults to \code{FALSE},
matching Stata's \code{ivreg2} defaults. With \code{small = FALSE}, inference uses
z and chi-squared statistics and the large-sample variance normalization.
Set \code{small = TRUE} for t and F statistics and the finite-sample \code{N - K}
correction, matching Stata's \code{small} option. \code{small} is the single, uniform
control for finite-sample corrections across every VCE type (\code{"iid"},
\code{"robust"}, \code{"HAC"}, \code{"AC"}); it is not a VCE selector. There are no
\code{"HC0"}/\code{"HC1"} values --- use \code{vcov = "robust"} and toggle \code{small}.
}

\section{Sigma (RMSE) normalization}{


Under \code{small = FALSE}, \eqn{\sigma^2 = RSS / (N - dofminus)}. Under
\code{small = TRUE}, \eqn{\sigma^2 = RSS / (N - K - dofminus - sdofminus)}. This
differs from \code{\link[=lm]{lm()}}, which always divides the residual sum of squares by its
residual degrees of freedom. Because ivreg2r reports the large-sample sigma
by default, its RMSE and any quantity built from sigma differ from \code{lm()}
unless \code{small = TRUE}.
}

\section{Standard errors relative to lm()}{


Coefficients match \code{\link[=lm]{lm()}} for OLS. Standard errors and the full
variance-covariance matrix match \code{lm()} only when \code{small = TRUE}; the
default \code{small = FALSE} reports Stata-convention standard errors without the
\code{N - K} finite-sample correction. Under analytic (\code{"aweight"}) or
probability (\code{"pweight"}) weights, sigma additionally differs from
\code{lm(..., weights = w)} by a factor of \code{sqrt(N / sum(w))}, because \code{lm()}
uses raw (unnormalized) weights while ivreg2r normalizes them (see below).
}

\section{Weight normalization}{


Analytic and probability weights are normalized internally to sum to \code{N},
following Stata. This makes sigma scale-invariant for those types:
multiplying every weight by a constant leaves all coefficients, standard
errors, and test statistics unchanged. Frequency (\code{"fweight"}) and
importance (\code{"iweight"}) weights are \emph{not} normalized; they redefine \code{N} as
\code{sum(weights)}, so their scale is meaningful by construction. Probability
weights force a robust VCE. See the \code{weight_type} argument of \code{\link[=ivreg2]{ivreg2()}}.
}

\section{Degrees-of-freedom adjustments}{


\code{dofminus} is a large-sample adjustment subtracted from \code{N} in large-sample
variance formulas (for example when fixed effects have been partialled out
upstream). \code{sdofminus} is a small-sample adjustment subtracted from the
residual degrees of freedom alongside \code{K} (for example the count of
regressors removed by \code{partial}). Both mirror Stata's \code{dofminus()} and
\code{sdofminus()} options. Stata's own source labels \code{dofminus} a large-sample
adjustment (e.g. number of fixed effects) and \code{sdofminus} a small-sample one
(e.g. number of partialled-out regressors).
}

\section{Confidence intervals and test statistics}{


\code{\link[=confint]{confint()}} and the model Wald test use the standard normal and chi-squared
distributions when \code{small = FALSE}, and the t and F distributions when
\code{small = TRUE}. The reported model test is therefore a chi-squared Wald
statistic by default and an F statistic under \code{small = TRUE}.
}

